Body:
> There will never be a "perfect" key binding as it depends on workflows used by each user, right? I think that is the case here. Agents have value in some context, but not others, and using for it by default didn't justify the use-case.

But why change it and break muscle memory and make it take an extra key press to switch agents just to make tab do nothing by default? There is no benefit to this change that I can tell, even if agents were useless and no muscle memory existed. Is there any discussions about the reason behind this change somewhere else?

@gontadu The v2 keybinds are here: https://opencode.ai/v2/docs/cli/keybinds

The `needs:compliance` label looks like a false positive — there is nothing in this report that asks anyone to run untrusted code. The only code is a CSS override you can paste into DevTools yourself, and it is entirely optional (a workaround, not a required step).

Short version of the fix: two CSS custom properties are declared with no CJK-capable family in the stack.

```css
/* out/renderer/assets/main-C-FJvlHS.css */
--font-family-text: "Inter", sans-serif;
--v2-font-family-sans: "Inter", sans-serif;
```

`Inter` is not bundled in `app.asar` and is not installed by default, so on a stock Windows install both variables collapse to the bare `sans-serif` generic, which does not walk the Windows font-linking chain to Microsoft YaHei. Since `BodyDesignClass` puts `var(--font-family-text)` on `<body>`, every glyph in the assistant message body that is not covered by Inter renders as `.notdef`. UI chrome is unaffected because it uses `--font-family-sans`, whose stack resolves through `system-ui`.

Suggested patch:

```css
--font-family-text: "Inter", "Microsoft YaHei", "Noto Sans SC", "PingFang SC", sans-serif;
--v2-font-family-sans: "Inter", "Microsoft YaHei", "Noto Sans SC", "PingFang SC", sans-serif;
```

Worth doing alongside that: have `setUIFont()` also write to `--font-family-text` / `--v2-font-family-sans`. Today the existing **Settings → General → UI Font** control only updates `--font-family-sans` and `--font-family-mono`, so users have no supported way to change the message body font.

Happy to test a build. For anyone who wants to confirm the diagnosis before a fix lands, changing the Windows default font to Microsoft YaHei or Noto Sans SC makes the boxes disappear, since the bare `sans-serif` generic then resolves to a font that has the glyphs.
